---
// PORTABLE: canonical copy - ncs-astro-sanity-starter is the library of record for this file
/* ============================================================================
   Analytics
   ============================================================================
   Foundation, edit with care.

   ONE FILE THAT ANSWERS "what analytics does this site have?". That is the
   whole point of it existing, and it is worth keeping even though the two
   conditions below would fit comfortably in BaseLayout.

   The failure this guards against is not a bug, it is an omission. A rebuild
   inherits the domain and the content but NOT the analytics tag, because the
   tag lived in whatever the old site was built in. It has cost this family
   twice: one site went dark for roughly six months when an Astro rebuild
   never carried the previous gtag snippet across, and another lost its
   analytics on the exact day its domain moved onto a Worker, having shipped
   with no measurement of any kind. Nothing failed either time. Every other
   cutover check passed. The number decayed over a thirty day window instead
   of dropping, so there was never a moment where it looked broken.

   A single named file is the cheapest fix for an omission: "open
   Analytics.astro" is a checkable step on a launch list, where "grep the
   layout for a tag" is not. Adding a third provider later adds a file here,
   not more conditions in a layout.

   WHY THE CONDITIONS LIVE HERE AND NOT IN BaseLayout.astro. This file carries
   the PORTABLE marker, so `npm run sync-check` holds every repo in the family
   to the same copy of it. BaseLayout.astro is deliberately NOT marked: it is
   per-repo and heavily customised in every site. Conditions placed there would
   be re-implemented eight times and would drift, which is how one repo in this
   family ended up with the raw vendor snippet under a different variable name.
   Keeping the gating in a marked file makes the drift detectable.

   BOTH TAGS ARE OPTIONAL AND SILENT WHEN ABSENT. An unset variable renders
   nothing at all. That is a deliberate contract, not an oversight: a fresh
   clone, a local dev server and a preview build all have to work with no
   analytics configured. It does mean a missing variable cannot announce
   itself, which is why carrying the tag over belongs on the cutover checklist
   next to DNS and TLS, and why studio-status watches for a flatline as the
   backstop.

   The PUBLIC_ prefix on both variables is required: these values are read in
   the browser. They are inlined at BUILD time because these pages are
   prerendered, so they must be set on the build environment, not the runtime
   one.

   See PORTS.md card 54.
   ============================================================================ */

import CloudflareBeacon from './analytics/CloudflareBeacon.astro';
import GoogleAnalytics from './analytics/GoogleAnalytics.astro';

// Cloudflare Web Analytics: a plain hex token from the Cloudflare dashboard.
const cfToken = (import.meta.env.PUBLIC_CF_ANALYTICS_TOKEN ?? '').trim();
const hasCfToken = cfToken.length > 0;

// Google Analytics 4: the web data stream Measurement ID (G-XXXXXXXXXX), NOT
// the numeric property id. The shape test is what makes a typo silent rather
// than a broken tag, and it is also what lets an empty value mean "no GA4".
const gaId = (import.meta.env.PUBLIC_GA_ID ?? '').trim();
const hasGa = /^G-[A-Z0-9]+$/.test(gaId);
---

{hasCfToken && <CloudflareBeacon token={cfToken} />}
{hasGa && <GoogleAnalytics gaId={gaId} />}
